  "response": "The question turns on two rival accounts of a single distribution, and researchers proceed by asking which account the available traces of the past can bear. Diffusion explanations attribute a shared practice to transmission between the two societies through contact, whereas independent invention explanations attribute a shared practice to separate origins in each society. That similar ecological constraints can produce convergent cultural traits in unconnected societies is strong evidence for the independent-invention account, since comparable subsistence problems can call forth comparable solutions among peoples who never met.\n\nChronology carries much of the evidential burden in such disputes. Because transmission from one society to another requires the practice to be older in the proposed source society, the relative sequence of first appearances becomes decisive, and radiocarbon dating of organic remains therefore establishes which society possessed the practice earlier. It is able to do so for the reason that radiocarbon dating measures the decay of potassium-40 in bone and shell, so that any organic residue associated with the practice can be assigned an age.\n\nQuantitative cross-cultural comparison introduces a complication that has shaped the statistical toolkit of the discipline. Galton's problem is the difficulty of treating culturally related societies as statistically independent cases. That very difficulty is why phylogenetic comparative methods control for shared linguistic ancestry when testing associations between cultural traits. Only with controls of that kind can a worldwide sample of societies be read as a set of informative trials rather than as a record of inherited similarity passed down within a single lineage.\n\nThe sweet potato in the Pacific is the standard worked example of how such inquiries are conducted. The Polynesian word \"kumara\" resembles words for sweet potato in Quechua. The sweet potato was domesticated in Southeast Asia. Traces of Indigenous South American ancestry are present in the genomes of some eastern Polynesian islanders. The lexical resemblance indicates that the Polynesian term for sweet potato derives from a South American language; a Southeast Asian domestication of the plant cannot be squared with a South American etymology for its Polynesian name, and at least one of the two findings holds, and perhaps both of them do: the genomic ancestry in the islands, or the South American source of the word for the crop.\n\nThe competing historical scenarios can be stated sharply enough to be adjudicated. Either no pre-Columbian contact occurred between Polynesians and the peoples of South America, or at least one episode of contact occurred between Polynesians and the peoples of South America before 1492; one of these must be the case and they cannot both be, so the accumulated linguistic, botanical, genetic and chronological evidence is marshalled precisely in order to determine which.",
